WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:13.735
in place, holding a basketball in one hand and a baseball in the other.

2
00:00:13.755 --> 00:00:20.919
"If the basketball is the sun and the baseball is Earth, the baseball should go around the basketball,"

3
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.000
he told himself.

4
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.482
"But why can't we see the sun all the time?"

5
00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:28.924
Dizzy, he lay down on his bed and stared at his homework.

6
00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:33.754
"All I know is that it gets dark at night—not why!"

7
00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:37.537
Will tried to focus on the worksheet, but he soon dozed off.

8
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.562
When he opened his eyes, he was amazed to see Earth and the moon before him.

9
00:00:45.582 --> 00:00:50.266
"Cool!" he exclaimed, pulling his phone, QT, out of his pocket.

10
00:00:50.286 --> 00:00:52.087
"Am I on a spaceship?"

11
00:00:53.128 --> 00:00:55.069
"Yes," QT replied.

12
00:00:55.089 --> 00:00:57.251
"Earth is beautiful, isn't it?"

13
00:00:57.271 --> 00:00:58.692
"It sure is.

14
00:00:59.596 --> 00:01:01.517
But why can I see only one side of it?"

15
00:01:02.037 --> 00:01:02.617
Will asked.

16
00:01:02.637 --> 00:01:04.338
"And of the moon too?"

17
00:01:04.358 --> 00:01:10.639
"That's because the sunlight's coming from the left, so it can't shine on the opposite side.

18
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.161
On the side that's lit right now, it is day.

19
00:01:14.721 --> 00:01:16.381
On the dark side, it is night.

20
00:01:16.841 --> 00:01:19.922
If you look carefully, you can see the lights of big cities."

21
00:01:19.942 --> 00:01:21.603
"I see them!

22
00:01:22.163 --> 00:01:24.984
But when the sun rises, the sides will switch, right?"

23
00:01:25.916 --> 00:01:29.199
"What you call the sun rising is not exactly correct.

24
00:01:29.699 --> 00:01:35.464
We don't experience day and night because the sun moves, but because Earth spins on its axis."

25
00:01:37.126 --> 00:01:38.107
Will was confused.

26
00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:39.528
"Its axis?

27
00:01:40.228 --> 00:01:40.769
What's that?"

28
00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:47.054
QT explained, "An axis is an imaginary line that goes through Earth's poles.

29
00:01:47.515 --> 00:01:48.135
I'll show you."

30
00:01:49.196 --> 00:01:53.600
QT shot a beam of light at the ship's controls, and a screen lit up one wall.

31
00:01:54.228 --> 00:01:59.389
"Earth rotates on its axis, spinning like a top, once every 24 hours.

32
00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:05.211
Although the rotation is too slow for us to see in real time, here's a video of the

33
00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:08.412
last 24 hours, sped up," QT said.

34
00:02:08.432 --> 00:02:12.693
"The planet is spinning!"

35
00:02:13.413 --> 00:02:14.053
Will observed.

36
00:02:14.073 --> 00:02:19.415
"But to us on Earth, it looks like the sun is rising in the east, moving across the

37
00:02:19.475 --> 00:02:21.375
sky, and setting in the west."

38
00:02:22.556 --> 00:02:24.512
"Exactly," QT replied.

39
00:02:24.532 --> 00:02:28.374
"The view from Earth is quite different from the view from space.

40
00:02:29.355 --> 00:02:33.516
Watch the moon in this next video, and you'll see what I mean," QT went on.

41
00:02:33.536 --> 00:02:42.140
"The moon's shape appears to change—what we call the phases of the moon—but it actually stays the same.

42
00:02:44.001 --> 00:02:46.482
What changes is how much of it we can see.

43
00:02:46.962 --> 00:02:50.244
Half the moon, the side facing the sun, is always lit.

44
00:02:51.588 --> 00:02:56.933
But because the moon orbits around Earth, we can usually only see part of the lit side."

45
00:02:56.953 --> 00:03:00.376
"Oh Will said, watching closely.

46
00:03:01.296 --> 00:03:06.240
After the video finished, he asked, "So where the moon is in its orbit determines how much of

47
00:03:06.301 --> 00:03:07.001
it we can see?

48
00:03:07.401 --> 00:03:09.023
That creates the phases of the moon?"

49
00:03:09.043 --> 00:03:12.686
"You've got it," QT answered.

50
00:03:12.706 --> 00:03:15.048
"But I want you to see something else.

51
00:03:15.548 --> 00:03:17.290
Take another look at Earth out there.

52
00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.772
Doesn't it look like it's tipping a little?"

53
00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:23.449
Staring at the planet, Will exclaimed, "Hey!

54
00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:24.369
It does."

55
00:03:24.389 --> 00:03:31.331
"Earth is tilted by about 23.5 degrees; that means it's not straight up and down.

56
00:03:31.851 --> 00:03:35.292
If we move farther out, you'll see what I mean," QT said.

57
00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:42.734
As they zoomed through the solar system, Earth got smaller and smaller, and the sun came into view.

58
00:03:45.915 --> 00:03:46.655
Will gasped.

59
00:03:47.235 --> 00:03:48.796
"It's enormous!

60
00:03:49.396 --> 00:03:49.916
And bright!"

61
00:03:50.888 --> 00:03:51.788
"It sure is.

62
00:03:52.308 --> 00:03:58.330
It looks small from Earth because it's very far away, about 150,000,000 kilometers.

63
00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.091
So the orbit is a long trip.

64
00:04:01.451 --> 00:04:09.553
It takes about 365 days to orbit the sun once—" "That's a year," Will interrupted.

65
00:04:09.573 --> 00:04:11.874
"Yes, it is.

66
00:04:12.414 --> 00:04:14.374
Earth's tilt causes the seasons.

67
00:04:14.874 --> 00:04:17.195
I'll show you a diagram," QT said.

68
00:04:17.695 --> 00:04:20.436
The screen lit up, showing Earth's orbit of the sun.

69
00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:27.262
"The planet is always tilted the same way, so the northern hemisphere is pointed toward the sun for

70
00:04:27.302 --> 00:04:30.544
part of the year, and the southern hemisphere for another part."

71
00:04:30.564 --> 00:04:35.966
"The seasons in the northern and southern hemispheres are always opposite?"

72
00:04:36.426 --> 00:04:36.926
Will asked.

73
00:04:38.007 --> 00:04:44.169
"Right. The hemisphere that's tilted away from the sun experiences winter, and the one that's tilted toward the

74
00:04:44.209 --> 00:04:45.670
sun experiences summer.

75
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:51.012
Right now it's December—winter in the northern hemisphere, where you live.

76
00:04:52.076 --> 00:04:55.659
See how little direct light it's getting compared to the southern hemisphere?

77
00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.083
There it's summer because it's getting a lot of direct light.

78
00:05:02.123 --> 00:05:07.867
When the northern hemisphere is tilted toward the sun, it gets much more sunlight, so it's summer.

79
00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:14.492
For spring and fall, neither hemisphere is tilted toward the sun, so the weather is the same."

80
00:05:16.393 --> 00:05:17.374
Will thought for a moment.

81
00:05:17.394 --> 00:05:20.136
"Is that why the days are longer during the summer?

82
00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:23.073
Because the northern hemisphere is tilted toward the sun?"

83
00:05:23.093 --> 00:05:28.737
"Absolutely. That's what causes the 24-hour days in the Arctic.

84
00:05:29.157 --> 00:05:34.460
And the Antarctic doesn't see the sun at all, causing 24-hour nights," QT said.

85
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.902
"But I'd better turn the ship around now.

86
00:05:38.282 --> 00:05:40.823
We don't want to be floating out in space forever, do we?"

87
00:05:41.864 --> 00:05:43.065
"No." Will grinned.

88
00:05:43.085 --> 00:05:45.306
"I've got basketball practice tomorrow!"

89
00:05:46.687 --> 00:05:51.853
The spaceship started moving back toward Earth, but as they flew past the sun, the ship swerved.

90
00:05:53.254 --> 00:05:54.015
"What's going on?"

91
00:05:54.455 --> 00:05:54.955
Will asked.

92
00:05:55.515 --> 00:05:58.797
Red lights on the controls were blinking, and a warning bell was ringing.

93
00:05:58.817 --> 00:06:04.260
"We might have gotten too close to the sun," QT said, sounding worried.

94
00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.501
"Its gravity is pulling us in!"

95
00:06:07.922 --> 00:06:10.964
The ship was going faster and faster, straight into the sun.

96
00:06:11.324 --> 00:06:14.746
Will covered his eyes with his hands to protect them from the blinding rays.

97
00:06:15.827 --> 00:06:20.557
When he lowered his hands, squinting at the bright light, his dad was standing by the bedroom door

98
00:06:20.597 --> 00:06:21.917
with his hand on the light switch.

99
00:06:21.937 --> 00:06:24.218
"Why was the light off?"

100
00:06:24.598 --> 00:06:25.298
he asked Will.

101
00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:29.420
"I was just napping," Will replied, crawling out of bed.

102
00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.280
"Thanks for waking me up.

103
00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:32.601
I have to finish something."

104
00:06:33.041 --> 00:06:35.462
He picked up his homework and quickly answered the questions.

105
00:06:35.482 --> 00:06:41.484
"That wasn't so hard," he thought, putting away the basketball and baseball.
